# Trivy suppressions for the CI image scans (Issues #131, #226).
#
# One file backs all four scans — finance-app, finance-migrate, finance-importer
# and finance-backup — so an entry here silences its CVE in EVERY image, not only
# the one that reported it. That is a deliberate simplification while the file is
# short: it keeps the single reviewable authority CONTRIBUTING.md links to. If
# entries start accumulating, split it into .trivy/<image>.trivyignore and point
# each scan step's `trivyignores:` at its own file, rather than letting one
# image's suppression widen to three that never needed it.
#
# The CI gate blocks on HIGH and CRITICAL findings that have a fix available
# (`ignore-unfixed: true`), so an entry here means "we know, and we have
# decided to ship anyway". That decision needs to be reviewable, so every
# entry must carry:
#
#   1. the CVE / advisory ID
#   2. why it does not apply, or why the fix cannot be taken yet
#   3. an expiry date — Trivy honours `exp:` and the finding returns after it
#
# Format:
#   CVE-YYYY-NNNNN exp:YYYY-MM-DD
#   # <why this is suppressed, and what unblocks removing it>
#
# Prefer rebuilding on a patched base image over adding an entry. If a
# suppression outlives two expiry extensions, that is a signal to change the
# base image rather than extend it a third time.
#
# Two of the four bases are not freely bumpable, which is worth knowing before
# reaching for an entry. postgres:18.6 (finance-backup) must stay version-matched
# to the server, so moving it means moving docker-compose.yml and the workflow
# service containers with it. Both it and python:3.14-slim (finance-importer) are
# rebuilt upstream under the same tag, so for an OS-package finding on either,
# re-running the job after upstream rebuilds is frequently the entire fix — check
# that before suppressing anything.
#
# Populate only from real findings, never pre-emptively.
#
# The entries below were seeded from the first scan of all four images (#226),
# which is the run that turned three previously unscanned images into gated ones.
# Every one of them is a base-image or bundled-tooling finding — none comes from
# code or a dependency this repo declares — and every one is scheduled for review
# on the same date, because the thing that clears most of them is the same event:
# an upstream rebuild of the base image.
#
# A second generation joined them in #291, and the widening this file's opening
# note warns about is now concrete rather than hypothetical: the util-linux
# entries silence those CVEs on the two Alpine images too, which do not report
# them today and would not report them if they later did. That is the price of
# one file backing four scans, and it is what the split described above buys back.
#
# NOTHING REMOVES AN ENTRY WHEN THE BASE IS REBUILT. The CVE simply stops being
# reported and the line stays here, inert, until someone deletes it — `exp:` stops
# the suppression working, it does not delete anything.
# scripts/check-trivy-suppressions.sh runs at the end of the image job and names
# the entries that no longer match a finding in any image; those are the ones to
# remove, along with their comment.

# --- OpenSSL, both base families (all four images) ---------------------------
CVE-2026-14456 exp:2026-10-06
# OpenSSL DoS through unbounded memory growth in the QUIC server listener.
# Reported against libcrypto3/libssl3 3.5.7-r0 on alpine 3.24.1 (finance-app,
# finance-migrate; fixed in 3.5.8-r0) and against libssl3t64/openssl/
# openssl-provider-legacy 3.5.6-1~deb13u2 on debian 13.6 (finance-importer,
# finance-backup; fixed in 3.5.7-1~deb13u2). It is the first entry in this file
# that genuinely belongs in every image, so the widening the header warns about
# does not apply here — all four report it, and all four are covered.
#
# WHY IT DOES NOT APPLY. The vulnerable code is the QUIC *server* path: an
# application that builds a Listener SSL object and accepts inbound QUIC
# connections with SSL_accept(). Initial packets for unknown connection IDs
# allocate per-channel state and queue it, unbounded, when the application does
# not accept fast enough. Reaching it requires *being* a QUIC server. These four
# images run `node server.js`, `migrate-and-seed.sh`, `python poll.py` and
# pg_dump/postgres — an HTTP/1.1 origin behind a reverse proxy, two psql clients
# and a file poller. None opens a QUIC listener; OpenSSL is reached here for
# client-side and TCP TLS only, and postgres has no QUIC support at all. (Node
# also links its own OpenSSL statically, so the alpine packages flagged above
# are the distro's, not the one node actually uses.) The single component in the
# stack that could speak HTTP/3 is caddy — a stock caddy:2.11.4-alpine this repo
# neither builds nor scans, started only under the `edge` profile, with no http3
# directive anywhere in caddy/, terminating TLS with Go's crypto/tls rather than
# OpenSSL.
#
# WHY NOT JUST REBUILD — checked before suppressing rather than after (#291).
# Unlike the util-linux batch below, "wait for the base to be rebuilt" has
# already been tried: all three bases were rebuilt within the last three days
# (node:24-alpine and python:3.14-slim 2026-08-27, postgres:18.6 2026-08-26) and
# the CI run on 2026-08-29 pulled them fresh and still found the vulnerable
# versions. The fix exists in both distros' package databases — that is what
# makes this a gating `fixed` finding rather than an unfixed one — but it had
# not reached those rebuilds. Re-running the job cannot clear it today.
#
# THE DATE IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE 2026-11-17 the entries below share. Those all
# clear on one Debian util-linux update. This one clears on an OpenSSL update
# that is already published in both distros and should arrive with the next base
# rebuild — weeks, not months — so a short date forces the re-check instead of
# parking it for a quarter. If it survives to 2026-10-06, that is real
# information: it means two distros' rebuilds have skipped a published fix
# twice, and the header's "change the base image" advice starts to apply.

# --- Debian base (finance-importer, finance-backup) -------------------------
CVE-2026-53615 exp:2026-11-17
# util-linux 2.41-5 in debian 13.6, reported against bsdutils/libblkid1/libmount1
# /libsmartcols1/libuuid1/liblastlog2-2/login/mount in both python:3.14-slim and
# postgres:18.6. Fixed in 2.41.5-0+deb13u1, which reaches us when those two base
# images are rebuilt upstream — nothing in this repo installs util-linux. Try
# re-running the job before extending this: the fix arrives on its own.
CVE-2026-53612 exp:2026-11-17
CVE-2026-53613 exp:2026-11-17
CVE-2026-53614 exp:2026-11-17
# The rest of the same util-linux batch (#291), against the same nine packages and
# carrying the SAME fixed version as CVE-2026-53615 above — 2.41.5-0+deb13u1. One
# Debian update clears all four, so they share its review date rather than getting
# an arbitrary one of their own; splitting the dates would assert a distinction
# that does not exist. Two TOCTOU races in mount(8) and a nosuid/noexec bypass via
# LIBMOUNT_FORCE_MOUNT2 — all three need a local user invoking mount, and these
# two images run one non-interactive process each and never mount anything.
# Verified before suppressing rather than after: at the time of writing, neither
# base had been rebuilt since the advisories landed (postgres:18.6 2026-08-15,
# python:3.14-slim 2026-08-12), so re-running the job could not have cleared it.

# --- python:3.14-slim bundled packages (finance-importer) -------------------
CVE-2025-47273 exp:2026-11-17
# setuptools 70.3.0, bundled in the base image rather than by
# importer/requirements.txt. poll.py never imports it; it is present because the
# image ships pip. Clears on a base rebuild, or could be removed outright if the
# importer image ever drops pip after the install layer.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 exp:2026-11-17
# msgpack 1.1.2, pulled in under the base image's pip tooling, not by
# requirements.txt. Same disposition as setuptools above.
#
# BOTH OF THESE WERE BRIEFLY DELETED IN #291 AND PUT STRAIGHT BACK, which is worth
# recording because the mistake is easy to repeat. A local run of
# scripts/check-trivy-suppressions.sh reported them stale, and a local
# `ls site-packages` appeared to confirm it. Both were wrong: the machine's cached
# python:3.14-slim was three weeks older than the one CI pulls, and these packages
# live under pip/_vendor/, not at the top level. CI failed on the very next push.
# Trust a staleness verdict from CI, where the image was just built from a freshly
# pulled base — not from a workstation.

# --- npm's own vendored dependencies (finance-migrate) ----------------------
# The migrate stage deliberately keeps npm — it runs `npx drizzle-kit` — where
# the runner stage deletes it precisely because of findings like these. They live
# in /usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/ and are fixed only when
# node:24-alpine ships a newer bundled npm; no application-level bump reaches
# them, and `npm update` inside the image would defeat the pinned build.
CVE-2026-59873 exp:2026-11-17
# tar 7.5.16 (CRITICAL), fixed in 7.5.19. npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-59874 exp:2026-11-17
# tar 7.5.16, fixed in 7.5.18. npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-13149 exp:2026-11-17
# brace-expansion 5.0.6, fixed in 5.0.7. npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-14257 exp:2026-11-17
# brace-expansion 5.0.6, fixed in 5.0.8. npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-69152 exp:2026-11-17
# brace-expansion 5.0.6, fixed in 5.0.9. npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-12151 exp:2026-11-17
# undici 6.26.0, fixed in 6.27.0. npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-69192 exp:2026-11-17
# ip-address 10.2.0, fixed in 10.3.1. npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-73566 exp:2026-11-17
# tar 7.5.16, fixed in 7.5.21. npm-vendored, as above (#291) — the same 7.5.16 the
# two tar entries at the top of this section already cover, so it clears with them
# when node:24-alpine ships a newer bundled npm.

# Not listed here, and excluded at the scanner instead: the Go stdlib CVEs
# reported against drizzle-kit's vendored `esbuild` and the postgres image's
# `gosu`. Both are stripped third-party binaries this repo neither compiles nor
# can patch, so they are `skip-files` entries on their scan steps in ci.yml with
# the reasoning recorded there. Suppressing them by ID would mean a new entry
# every time the Go project publishes a stdlib advisory, forever.
